  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry through Monday with worsening drought \nconditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nDry conditions continue, with no precipitation forecast today\nand Monday. High pressure remains centered to our south, \nbringing breezy daytime winds across the region on both days. \nThe high pressure will continue to pipe warm air into the region\nfrom the southwest. This results in a warming trend today and\nMonday, with highs in the 50s today, and upper 50s in the north,\nand low-to-mid 60s Downeast on Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances return Monday night and remain \nthrough the rest of the week, but very low confidence on whether\nor not there will be notable drought relief.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure over the Great Lakes moves east, bringing a \nfrontal system that stalls over the northeast Monday into Monday\nnight. Small shortwave on Monday results a chance for light \nshowers in the north beginning Monday night, continuing into \nTuesday. Very minimal accumulation with these showers. Tuesday \ninto Tuesday night, backdoor cold front pushes through the\nnorth, providing another chance for rain. Anticipating roughly \n0.30 inches or less to fall in the north, tapering down to lower\naccumulations as you move from northern Aroostook county.\n\nWednesday onwards, confidence in the forecast becomes low. \nModels are struggling with the placement of a low pressure \nsystem in the plains, and whether or not rain will make it into \nMaine. Some ensembles/models have decent rain, while others have\nnone. Heading toward the weekend, there is a some better \nconfidence in a low pressure system moving into the region from \nthe southeast bringing some moisure, but too early to determine\nexact timing and location.",
